│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ heap_based_balanced_packing_speedup                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ The main bottleneck of the algorithm (times_algorithm = 0.56) is `balanced_packing`: it   │
│                          │ uses a pure-Python O(n · num_packs) scan per layer to find the least-loaded pack with     │
│                          │ remaining capacity. Replace this linear scan with a min-heap keyed by (load, pack_id):    │
│                          │ each item assignment costs O(log num_packs) instead of O(num_packs), and the heap         │
│                          │ automatically enforces the "exactly n/m objects per pack" capacity constraint (a pack is  │
│                          │ simply not pushed back once full). Additionally, the per-element tensor writes from       │
│                          │ Python are very slow; we buffer the (group, pack) assignments per layer in Python lists   │
│                          │ and perform a single vectorized indexed write into the tensors per layer. This keeps the  │
│                          │ same greedy LPT-with-cardinality-constraint packing quality (so balancedness is           │
│                          │ unchanged) while dramatically cutting the Python-side execution time, which improves the  │
│                          │ speed score and hence the combined score.                                                 │
