│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ vectorize_balanced_packing_snake                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ The main bottleneck is `balanced_packing`: it contains a pure-Python double loop over all │
│                          │ layers × all groups, doing a linear scan of pack weights for every item (O(num_layers *   │
│                          │ num_groups * num_packs)). This makes `speed_score` 0.00. I replace it with a fully        │
│                          │ vectorized "snake" (serpentine) LPT-style assignment: sort items descending once per      │
│                          │ layer, then assign the sorted sequence round-robin with alternating direction. This       │
│                          │ guarantees each pack receives exactly n/m items, keeps sums well balanced (snake pairing  │
│                          │ strong items with weak items beats plain round-robin), and runs in O(n log n) fully on    │
│                          │ tensor ops with no Python loop. Since the hierarchical algorithm is dominated by this     │
│                          │ routine (called twice with large row counts, e.g. num_layers*num_nodes rows), this should │
│                          │ dramatically cut execution time while preserving/improving balancedness.                  │
│                          │ `replicate_experts` is already vectorized; the remaining ops are cheap tensor kernels.    │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
