╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ vectorized_packing_with_local_search_refinement                                           │
│ patch_description        │ The current `balanced_packing` runs a pure-Python double loop (one iteration per layer ×  │
│                          │ per expert group) with a Python `min(...)` over packs for every single item, which        │
│                          │ dominates the algorithm's runtime (times_algorithm score 0.56, speed_score 0.00). It also │
│                          │ produces unbalanced packs because it never revisits its greedy decisions.                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Two changes, both inside `balanced_packing`:                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Vectorized greedy best-fit across layers**: instead of looping over layers and items │
│                          │ in Python (O(num_layers × num_groups) Python iterations, each doing a Python `min` over   │
│                          │ packs), we loop only over the item-step `t` (num_groups iterations) and process all       │
│                          │ layers at once with `masked_fill`/`argmin`/`scatter_add`. This removes the per-layer      │
│                          │ Python loop entirely and turns the inner pack-selection into one vectorized op per step.  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Local-search refinement for better balance**: after the greedy pass, run a bounded   │
│                          │ (8 rounds) vectorized refinement that swaps the heaviest item of the currently-heaviest   │
│                          │ pack with the lightest item of the currently-lightest pack whenever it strictly reduces   │
│                          │ the pack-weight spread (`2*(w_heavy - w_light) > spread`). Since packs always hold an     │
│                          │ equal number of items, swapping preserves the cardinality constraint while reducing the   │
│                          │ makespan. Ranks are recomputed at the end via a stable sort (each pack occupies exactly   │
│                          │ `groups_per_pack` consecutive sorted positions, so ranks follow arithmetically).          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This improves both goals: pack weights are measurably more balanced (better balancedness  │
│                          │ of per-GPU loads, since both Step 1 group-packing and Step 3 physical-expert-packing call │
│                          │ this function), and execution is much faster (no per-item Python work, no `.cpu()` sync,  │
│                          │ no Python `min` scans).                                                                   │
