                        Patch Metadata - Gen 10/100 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ vectorized_lpt_balancer                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The current implementation runs a pure-Python double loop in `balanced_packing` (over     │
│                          │ every layer × every expert), making the balancer itself the bottleneck. The new approach  │
│                          │ keeps the same overall problem decomposition (node packing → replication → GPU packing)   │
│                          │ but replaces all per-layer Python loops with fully vectorized tensor operations that      │
│                          │ process all layers simultaneously. Balanced packing is done as a vectorized LPT           │
│                          │ (longest-processing-time-first) greedy: items are visited in globally sorted order, and   │
│                          │ for each position the destination pack is chosen with a single masked `argmin` across all │
│                          │ layers at once. Replication is a vectorized water-filling greedy on `weight/cnt` (already │
│                          │ minimizing the max replica load, which directly improves expert-level balancedness).      │
│                          │ Everything stays on the GPU (no `.cpu()` round-trips), which removes the host-device sync │
│                          │ cost that dominated the original runtime. The result is the same or better packing        │
│                          │ quality with dramatically lower algorithm execution time.                                 │
