│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ snake_packing_swap_refinement                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The current `balanced_packing` uses a purely static snake (boustrophedon) assignment of   │
│                          │ sorted items to packs. While fast and decent, it ignores the actual accumulated pack      │
│                          │ loads. I propose adding a vectorized local-search refinement pass after the snake         │
│                          │ assignment: repeatedly identify (per layer, fully vectorized with gathers/scatters) the   │
│                          │ most-loaded pack and the least-loaded pack, take the *lightest* item from the heavy pack  │
│                          │ and the *heaviest* item from the light pack, and swap their (pack, rank) assignments when │
│                          │ the swap provably shrinks the max-min load spread (condition: `w_heavy > w_light` and     │
│                          │ `spread > 2*(w_heavy - w_light)`). Each swap keeps every pack's cardinality intact        │
│                          │ (required by the algorithm's contract of exactly n/m objects per pack) and preserves the  │
│                          │ bijection between (pack, rank) slots, so downstream `inverse()` mappings still work.      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This hill-climbing refinement directly reduces the per-GPU load spread (improving         │
│                          │ `balancedness_score_gpu`) while remaining fully vectorized across all layers, so the      │
│                          │ runtime overhead is a handful of small tensor ops per iteration (bounded, e.g., 64        │
│                          │ iterations) — keeping `times_algorithm` low since the algorithm time is dominated by the  │
│                          │ replication loop. No other function signatures or outputs change.                         │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
