╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ heap_lpt_fast_packing                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The current `balanced_packing` runs a Python-level loop over all items, but each          │
│                          │ iteration performs several full-row tensor operations (`torch.where`, `argmin`, two       │
│                          │ `scatter_`, fancy indexing). That is O(n) tensor kernel launches per row — the dominant   │
│                          │ cost of the whole algorithm (it is called twice per rebalance). Replacing it with a       │
│                          │ classic heapq-based LPT packer operating on plain Python lists removes all per-item       │
│                          │ tensor kernel launches while producing an identical (or better, due to deterministic      │
│                          │ tie-breaking) assignment. Since weights are already moved to CPU as the first operation   │
│                          │ of `rebalance_experts`, pure-Python list arithmetic is very fast at these problem sizes   │
│                          │ (tens of layers, hundreds of experts). Likewise, `replicate_experts` performs             │
│                          │ `num_redundant` iterations of tensor ops (`argmax`, gather, index updates); converting it │
│                          │ to a per-row Python greedy with lists eliminates those kernel launches too. This keeps    │
│                          │ the exact same greedy semantics (clone the expert with the highest per-replica load) but  │
│                          │ with far lower overhead, directly improving `times_algorithm` / `speed_score` without     │
│                          │ touching the resulting balancedness scores. Per the reliability recommendation, `weight = │
│                          │ weight.float().cpu()` stays the first operation in the entry point, all intermediate      │
│                          │ tensors remain on CPU, and `torch.sort(..., descending=True)` (no newer kwargs) is used   │
│                          │ for the initial ordering.                                                                 │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0191                                                                                   │
│ error_attempt            │ None                                                                                      │
│ system_prompt_id         │ None                                                                                      │
│ model_name               │ z-ai/glm-5.3                                                                              │
│ temperature              │ 1.0                                                                                       │
│ max_output_tokens        │ 16384                                                                                     │
│ reasoning                │ {'effort': 'low'}                                                                         │
│ diff_summary             │ added: 20; deleted: 0; modified: 42;                                                      │
