│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ cap_and_spread_replica_counts                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The expert balancedness score is stuck at 0.25 across all prior programs, while GPU       │
│                          │ balancedness reaches 0.66. The diagnosis in the recommendation is that the greedy         │
│                          │ replication loop in `replicate_experts` concentrates all redundant replicas on a handful  │
│                          │ of hottest experts (argmax of `weight/logcnt` repeatedly picks the same experts until     │
│                          │ their per-replica load drops), producing extreme replica-count variance: a few experts    │
│                          │ hold many replicas while the `maxlogcnt` headroom is wasted. Packing cannot compensate    │
│                          │ for that variance since a single hot expert with 1 replica dominates its GPU.             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix: cap each logical expert's replica count at `maxcnt = num_redundant // 2 + 1` and │
│                          │ force the greedy loop to spread extra replicas to the next-highest per-replica-load       │
│                          │ experts that are still below the cap. This keeps the exact same bookkeeping invariants    │
│                          │ (`phy2log`, `rank = logcnt[...]` before increment, `logcnt` increment), so the downstream │
│                          │ `phy2log * maxlogcnt + phyrank` scatter never goes out of bounds (the invariant Gen 6     │
│                          │ broke), because `maxcnt <= num_redundant + 1 = maxlogcnt` always holds. The capacity      │
│                          │ argument is safe: `num_log * (maxcnt - 1) >= num_redundant` whenever `num_log >= 2`, so   │
│                          │ there is always at least one eligible expert and the masked argmax never picks an         │
│                          │ excluded entry. Execution cost is identical to the current loop (same number of           │
│                          │ vectorized iterations), and better balance also reduces inference time.                   │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0229                                                                                   │
