│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ cap_replica_spread                                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The current `replicate_experts` uses a pure greedy argmax loop that gives replica after   │
│                          │ replica to the currently-highest-load expert (by weight/count). This produces extreme     │
│                          │ replica-count spreads (one hot expert gets most of the redundant budget, most experts     │
│                          │ stay at 1), which hurts the expert balancedness score (0.25) and leaves the packing step  │
│                          │ with highly skewed per-replica loads. To improve balance, cap the maximum replica count   │
│                          │ per logical expert at `num_redundant // 2 + 1` (but never below the feasibility floor     │
│                          │ `ceil(num_phy / num_log)`, so the full physical budget can always be allocated). Once an  │
│                          │ expert hits the cap it is excluded from further selection, so excess replicas are spread  │
│                          │ to the next-highest-load experts instead of piling onto one expert. This keeps all counts │
│                          │ within a bounded range, so the downstream `phy2log * maxlogcnt + phyrank` scatter         │
│                          │ invariant (the full-budget `maxlogcnt = num_redundant + 1` used in `rebalance_experts`)   │
│                          │ still comfortably holds: with the cap, the maximum observed rank is strictly smaller, so  │
│                          │ no out-of-bounds scatter can occur. The change adds only a `torch.where` mask per         │
│                          │ iteration, so runtime is unchanged (same O(num_redundant) vectorized iterations).         │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0133                                                                                   │
│ error_attempt            │ None                                                                                      │
│ system_prompt_id         │ None                                                                                      │
